toBracketPolynomial -- Represent an invariant polynomial as a polynomial in brackets
************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        g = toBracketPolynomial(f,B)
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, 
      * B, an instance of the type *note BracketRing: BracketRing,, 
  * Outputs:
      * g, an instance of the type *note GCExpression: GCExpression,, 

Description
===========

A polynomial invariant under the action of the special linear group may be
represented by a polynomial in brackets. See *note Bracket: Bracket, for more
details. Groebner basis methods allow one to compute such a representation.
Below is a simple example.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : B = bracketRing(3, 2)                                                   |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = B                                                                       |
|      3,2                                                                    |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : BracketRing                                                             |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : X = matrix B                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = {-1} | x_(1,1) x_(1,2) |                                                |
|     {-1} | x_(2,1) x_(2,2) |                                                |
|     {-1} | x_(3,1) x_(3,2) |                                                |
|                                                                             |
|                                                 3                           |
|o2 : Matrix (QQ[x   ..x   , y    , y    , y    ])  <-- (QQ[x   ..x   , y    ,|
|                 1,1   3,2   [23]   [13]   [12]             1,1   3,2   [23] |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|              2                                                              |
|y    , y    ])                                                               |
| [13]   [12]                                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = (det X^{1,2}) - (det X^{0,2}) + (det X^{0,1})                       |
|                                                                             |
|o3 = - x   x    + x   x    + x   x    - x   x    - x   x    + x   x          |
|        1,2 2,1    1,1 2,2    1,2 3,1    2,2 3,1    1,1 3,2    2,1 3,2       |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : QQ[x   ..x   , y    , y    , y    ]                                     |
|         1,1   3,2   [23]   [13]   [12]                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : toBracketPolynomial(f,B)                                                |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = [23]-[13]+[12]                                                          |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : GCExpression                                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Such a representation is not unique. It may be checked that two bracket
polynomials are equal through their normal form with respect to a Groebner
basis. See *note normalForm: normalForm, for a further explanation.

See also *note BracketRing: BracketRing,.

Ways to use toBracketPolynomial:
================================

  * "toBracketPolynomial(RingElement,BracketRing)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note toBracketPolynomial: toBracketPolynomial, is a *note method
function: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

